1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:09,390
On 23 October 1956, students in Budapest marched with Hungarian flags that had a hole in the middle.

2
00:00:09,470 --> 00:00:15,180
They had cut out the Communist crest. By midnight, their march had become an uprising.

3
00:00:15,260 --> 00:00:23,080
For twelve days, Hungarians fought, ran their own towns and factories, and forced a new government.

4
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:39,790
The day before, students at the Technical University had drawn up 16 demands: Soviet troops out, free elections, Imre Nagy in government.

5
00:00:39,870 --> 00:00:49,330
Their march, in sympathy with Poland's reforms, was banned, then allowed. At 3 p.m. it set off from Pest and from Buda.

6
00:00:49,410 --> 00:00:55,120
The two columns met at the statue of General Bem, and there the flag with the hole appeared.

7
00:00:55,200 --> 00:01:05,690
By 6 p.m. the crowd before Parliament was at least 200,000, perhaps 300,000 strong, the UN committee estimated.

8
00:01:05,770 --> 00:01:13,140
At 8, the party leader, Ernő Gerő, condemned "nationalist demonstrations" on the radio.

9
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:19,360
About 9, Imre Nagy spoke briefly from a balcony and asked the crowd to go home.

10
00:01:19,440 --> 00:01:26,260
At about 9.30, by the City Park, a crowd pulled down the huge Stalin statue.

11
00:01:26,340 --> 00:01:32,980
At the Radio, guarded by the ÁVH secret police, students wanted their demands broadcast.

12
00:01:33,060 --> 00:01:40,080
Shortly after 9 p.m., tear gas and then shots came from the building, and people in the street were killed.

13
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:48,350
That the firing began then is documented. Who fired the very first shot, and on whose order, is still disputed.

14
00:01:48,430 --> 00:01:57,820
Soldiers sent to help the ÁVH joined the crowd, workers brought arms, and before dawn the first Soviet tanks rolled in.

15
00:02:01,020 --> 00:02:10,860
The tanks came from Soviet bases in Hungary itself: about 20,000 troops and 600 tanks, a UN inquiry found.

16
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:19,960
In the morning, the radio named Imre Nagy prime minister and announced martial law in his name. He later said he had not signed it.

17
00:02:20,040 --> 00:02:27,680
Against the tanks stood armed groups formed overnight, mostly young workers and students, some barely past childhood.

18
00:02:27,760 --> 00:02:34,650
The biggest held the Corvin köz, a passage round a cinema in Pest's 8th district, ringed by tall houses.

19
00:02:34,730 --> 00:02:42,190
Across the avenue, soldiers in the Kilián barracks under Colonel Pál Maléter took the insurgents' side.

20
00:02:42,270 --> 00:02:50,890
The groups struck at tanks on the wide avenues, then withdrew into side streets where tanks with little infantry could not follow.

21
00:02:50,970 --> 00:02:59,430
In Buda, the group at Széna tér was led by János Szabó, a truck driver of nearly sixty, known as Uncle Szabó.

22
00:02:59,510 --> 00:03:06,030
From Csepel, the factory island in the south, workers came north to help the fighters in the city.

23
00:03:06,110 --> 00:03:14,430
Soviet soldiers had been told to crush "counter-revolutionary gangs". People argued with them in the Russian they had learnt at school.

24
00:03:14,510 --> 00:03:22,650
The young paid dearly. Of all who died in the fighting that autumn, more than one in five was under twenty.

25
00:03:25,850 --> 00:03:37,140
On Thursday 25 October, people near the Astoria talked with Soviet tank crews; some climbed aboard, and together they went to Parliament to see Imre Nagy.

26
00:03:37,220 --> 00:03:44,230
Between 11 and noon, the unarmed crowd in Kossuth Square was fired on from several directions.

27
00:03:44,310 --> 00:03:53,900
Who fired is still argued over: Soviet tanks in the side streets, shooters on the rooftops, or Hungarian border guards.

28
00:03:53,980 --> 00:04:02,120
The number of dead is just as disputed. The regime's White Book said 22; at least 75 are known by name.

29
00:04:02,200 --> 00:04:12,860
The UN report gave estimates of 300 to 800, and some witnesses spoke of a thousand. No one was ever called to account.

30
00:04:12,940 --> 00:04:19,130
That day the party dropped Ernő Gerő as its leader and put János Kádár in his place.

31
00:04:19,210 --> 00:04:27,670
The next day at Mosonmagyaróvár, near the Austrian border, border guards fired on an unarmed crowd outside their barracks.

32
00:04:27,750 --> 00:04:39,650
The 1956 Institute counts 52 dead; reports at the time said up to 105. In revenge, the crowd killed several officers.

33
00:04:42,850 --> 00:04:53,340
Outside Budapest there was little fighting at first. In most places, power passed without a fight to revolutionary councils, chosen at public meetings.

34
00:04:53,420 --> 00:05:01,580
From 24 October, factories and mines elected workers' councils, and a general strike spread across the country.

35
00:05:01,660 --> 00:05:11,810
In Győr, on 30 October, about 400 delegates formed the Transdanubian National Council, which called for neutrality.

36
00:05:15,010 --> 00:05:23,280
On 27 October, Nagy brought two Smallholders, Zoltán Tildy and Béla Kovács, into a new government.

37
00:05:23,360 --> 00:05:32,850
At dawn on 28 October, a Soviet attack on the Corvin köz was beaten back, and that day the government ordered a ceasefire.

38
00:05:32,930 --> 00:05:42,270
On the radio, Nagy refused to call the uprising a counter-revolution. It was, he said, "a great national and democratic movement".

39
00:05:42,350 --> 00:05:52,120
The ÁVH was dissolved on 29 October. On the 30th, Nagy announced the end of one-party rule, and free elections were promised.

40
00:05:52,200 --> 00:05:59,260
Soviet tanks began to leave Budapest, and Moscow declared that it respected the sovereignty of its allies.

41
00:05:59,340 --> 00:06:07,930
The same day, armed insurgents besieged the Budapest party headquarters on Republic Square, believing prisoners were held there.

42
00:06:08,010 --> 00:06:13,720
Its defenders were young ÁVH conscripts, party staff and army officers.

43
00:06:13,800 --> 00:06:20,990
When it fell, the men who came out to negotiate were shot. Then the crowd shot or lynched several of the defenders.

44
00:06:21,070 --> 00:06:32,360
In all, 23 defenders died, by the 1956 Institute's count, and attackers died too. Revolutionary organisations condemned the lynchings.

45
00:06:32,440 --> 00:06:40,380
After 4 November, the Kádár regime held up these killings as proof of a "counter-revolution".

46
00:06:43,580 --> 00:06:52,370
On 31 October, as the tanks left, Nagy told a crowd: "we are living in the first days of our sovereignty and independence".

47
00:06:52,450 --> 00:07:00,770
That day in Moscow, in secret, Khrushchev told the Soviet leaders: "We should not withdraw our troops from Hungary and Budapest."

48
00:07:00,850 --> 00:07:08,040
Leaving, he said, would look like weakness to the West, just as Britain and France were going to war with Egypt.

49
00:07:08,120 --> 00:07:15,460
A new government loyal to Moscow would be set up, and the army was given three days to prepare.

50
00:07:15,540 --> 00:07:22,250
On 1 November, Nagy learned that new Soviet divisions were crossing Hungary's eastern border.

51
00:07:22,330 --> 00:07:28,470
The Soviet ambassador, Yuri Andropov, told him the troops were there to cover the withdrawal.

52
00:07:28,550 --> 00:07:36,920
That evening Nagy declared Hungary neutral, out of the Warsaw Pact, and asked the United Nations to defend its neutrality.

53
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:46,090
Later the radio broadcast János Kádár praising the people's "glorious uprising". But Kádár had already gone over to the Soviets.

54
00:07:46,170 --> 00:07:54,760
At Szolnok, the Warsaw Pact commander, Marshal Ivan Konev, gave the order to attack on 4 November.

55
00:07:54,840 --> 00:08:02,450
On 3 November, Nagy formed a coalition of four parties, with Pál Maléter as minister of defence.

56
00:08:02,530 --> 00:08:10,140
At noon, talks on the withdrawal began in Parliament, and that evening they went on at the Soviet base at Tököl.

57
00:08:10,220 --> 00:08:19,270
At about 10 p.m., KGB officers led by Ivan Serov arrested Maléter and his delegation.

58
00:08:22,670 --> 00:08:30,810
In twelve days, Hungarians had ended one-party rule, dissolved the ÁVH, and run their own towns and factories.

59
00:08:30,890 --> 00:08:38,410
Students and workers, young fighters and old, soldiers and neighbours did it together, and many paid with their lives.

60
00:08:38,490 --> 00:08:45,780
Not every death came in battle. Unarmed crowds were fired on, and men of the ÁVH were lynched.

61
00:08:45,860 --> 00:08:52,320
On Saturday night, 3 November, Budapest went to sleep hoping for a quiet Sunday.

62
00:08:52,400 --> 00:08:57,140
Before dawn, the Soviet tanks began to move.
